  "chooseSection": {
    "heading": "How to Choose a Stash Alternative",
    "body": "Start with the balance you plan to hold, because flat fees and percentage fees cost different amounts at different balances. Stash, Acorns, Robinhood Gold and Betterment's base price charge a flat monthly amount. Wealthfront, Fidelity Go from $25,000 and Betterment after qualifying deposits charge a percentage of assets.\n\nStash combines both models. On a $10,000 Smart Portfolio, Stash costs $108 plus 0.25% of $10,000 ($25), or $133 a year on annual billing. On monthly billing, the same account costs $144 plus $25, or $169 a year.\n\nThat combination sets two break-even points. Wealthfront charges the same 0.25% rate, so a Stash Smart Portfolio of $1,000 or more costs $108 more per year than Wealthfront at the same balance on annual billing. Fidelity Go charges 0.35% from $25,000, which is 0.10% more than Stash's rate, and 0.10% of $108,000 is $108, so Fidelity Go costs less than a Stash Smart Portfolio below $108,000 and more above it.\n\nNext, price the extras. Stash, Acorns Gold and Robinhood Gold each list a 3% retirement or IRA match capped at $225. Acorns limits its Gold match to new contributions in the first year, and Robinhood requires one year on Gold and five years of IRA assets for the full match.\n\nCash yield is a separate line in the comparison. Robinhood Gold lists 3.6% APY on eligible brokerage cash, Acorns Emergency Savings lists 3.59%, Betterment Cash Reserve lists 3.50% and the M1 High-Yield Cash Account lists 3.35%. Acorns and Betterment describe their rates as variable, and the Stash pricing page lists no cash APY.\n\nLast, match the account type. Stash offers personal brokerage, retirement, custodial accounts for kids and Smart Portfolios. M1 adds joint, trust, Traditional IRA, Roth IRA and Simplified Employee Pension (SEP) IRA accounts, while Fidelity Go covers IRA, HSA and taxable accounts."
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "heading": "Stash vs Acorns Pricing by Tier",
      "body": "Acorns sells three monthly tiers: Bronze at $4, Silver at $8 and Gold at $12. Every tier includes an investment account with an expert-built diversified portfolio, Round-Ups, the Acorns Later retirement account, Earn bonus investments and Learn courses. Acorns Checking lists no overdraft fees and 55,000+ fee-free automated teller machines (ATMs).\n\nSilver adds a 1% IRA match on new contributions in the first year. Gold raises that match to 3%, up to $225 on a maxed annual contribution according to Acorns. Gold also adds Early Invest for kids, Custom Portfolios of individual stocks and ETFs, a $10,000 life insurance policy for eligible customers and a no-cost Will valued at $299.\n\nAcorns Gold fits people who want Stash-style perks plus Round-Ups, checking, tax filing through third-party providers, and allowance and chore tools. As an illustration on list prices, Acorns Gold costs $12 x 12 = $144 per year, the same as Stash on monthly billing. Stash billed annually costs $108, which is $36 less than Acorns Gold.\n\nAcorns Bronze costs $4 x 12 = $48 per year, which is $60 less than Stash on annual billing. Bronze carries no IRA match, because the match starts at Silver. Acorns Silver costs $8 x 12 = $96 per year for a 1% first-year match.\n\nAcorns falls short of Stash on billing options and match timing. Acorns lists only monthly tier prices, while Stash offers the $108 annual price. Acorns lists its Gold match on new contributions in the first year, while Stash describes its 3% match as worth up to $225 per year."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Betterment Base Price and Percentage Fee",
      "body": "Betterment charges a base price of $5 per month on investing accounts, which is $5 x 12 = $60 per year. The fee switches to 0.25% a year once recurring deposits total $200 or more per month or balances reach $24,000 or more across Betterment investing accounts. Betterment has no minimum balance, and fund expenses are charged in addition to Betterment's fees.\n\nBetterment Premium costs 0.65% a year, requires $100,000 in eligible investments and adds access to advisors. Balances over $1 million get lower rates: 0.15% for $1 million to $2 million and 0.10% above $2 million. Cash Reserve lists a 3.50% variable APY as of 9/21/2026, plus an added 0.75% on balances up to $1 million for new clients with a qualifying deposit.\n\nBetterment Checking lists no overdraft fees and reimburses ATM fees. Cash Reserve lists no fees.\n\nBetterment fits people who want a percentage fee with no separate subscription once deposits qualify. As an illustration, a $10,000 balance with $200 in monthly deposits costs 0.25% of $10,000, or $25 a year, before fund expenses. The same balance in a Stash Smart Portfolio costs $133 a year on annual billing, a gap of $108.\n\nBetterment falls short of Stash on bundled perks. Betterment's pricing lists no retirement match, life insurance policy or stock rewards, while The Stash Plan includes all three. At $100,000, Betterment Premium costs 0.65% of $100,000, or $650 a year, against $108 plus $250, or $358, for a Stash Smart Portfolio on annual billing."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Wealthfront Advisory Fee and Fee-Free Brokerage",
      "body": "Wealthfront charges a 0.25% annual advisory fee on the Wealthfront Portfolio, with no monthly subscription. Wealthfront describes tax-loss harvesting as covering that fee for many Classic portfolio clients. The Wealthfront Brokerage Account lists no fees and $1 to get started, for buying ETFs and stocks without advisory or management fees.\n\nWealthfront fits people who want a managed portfolio priced only as a percentage, or a fee-free account for buying ETFs and stocks. As an illustration, a $10,000 Wealthfront Portfolio costs 0.25% of $10,000, or $25 a year. A $10,000 Stash Smart Portfolio costs $133 a year on annual billing, so Wealthfront costs $108 less at that balance.\n\nAt $50,000, the Wealthfront Portfolio fee is 0.25% of $50,000, or $125 a year. A $50,000 Stash Smart Portfolio costs $108 plus $125, or $233 a year on annual billing. The $108 gap stays the same at every balance, because both charge 0.25%.\n\nWealthfront falls short of Stash on extras bundled into the price. Wealthfront's pricing lists a Cash Account that earns an APY, but no retirement match, life insurance policy or stock rewards. The [Wealthfront alternatives](/alternatives/wealthfront/) comparison covers providers priced against Wealthfront."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Robinhood Gold Price and IRA Match",
      "body": "Robinhood Gold costs $5 per month, which is $5 x 12 = $60 per year, and the first 30 days are free. Gold pays 3.6% APY on eligible brokerage cash with no cap and no minimum. Gold also adds a 3% match on IRA contributions, up to $225 based on the 2026 maximum.\n\nThe full Robinhood match requires one year on Gold and five years of assets in the IRA. Gold also allows deposits up to 3x portfolio value and offers Robinhood Strategies managed portfolios with no management fees above $100,000 for Gold members. Futures cost $0.50 per contract, index options cost $0.35 per contract and instant withdrawal costs a 1.5% fee.\n\nRobinhood Gold fits people who want an IRA match the same size as Stash's at a lower subscription price. On published prices, Gold costs $60 a year against $108 for Stash billed annually, a $48 gap, or $84 less than Stash billed monthly. As an illustration, $5,000 of eligible brokerage cash at 3.6% APY would earn $180 over a year if the rate held.\n\nRobinhood falls short of Stash on life insurance and stock rewards. The Stash Plan includes a $10,000 life insurance policy and Stock-Back rewards worth up to $120 per year, and Robinhood Gold lists neither. Stash describes itself as built for long-term investing and says it is not built for day trading.\n\nInstant withdrawal adds a cost Stash does not list: a $1,000 instant withdrawal at 1.5% costs $15. The [Robinhood Gold breakdown](/guides/is-robinhood-gold-worth-it/) and the [Robinhood alternatives](/alternatives/robinhood/) guide cover Gold in more depth."
    },
    {
      "heading": "M1 Platform Fee and Account Types",
      "body": "M1 charges no commission, trading or management fees on self-directed brokerage accounts. M1 adds a $3 monthly platform fee, which M1 waives for clients with more than $10,000 at M1. Other fees may apply, including regulatory, account closure and American depositary receipt fees.\n\nM1 Advisor is a free advisory service for M1 clients. The M1 High-Yield Cash Account lists 3.35% APY on uninvested cash. Margin is available on accounts with at least $2,000 in eligible securities.\n\nM1 account types include individual, joint, custodial, Traditional IRA, Roth IRA, SEP IRA and trust accounts. Stash lists personal brokerage, retirement, custodial and Smart Portfolio accounts. M1 fits people who need a joint, trust or SEP IRA account alongside self-directed investing.\n\nAs an illustration, an $8,000 M1 account pays $3 x 12 = $36 a year. An $8,000 Stash Smart Portfolio costs $108 plus 0.25% of $8,000 ($20), or $128, on annual billing. At $15,000, M1 waives its platform fee, while the Stash Smart Portfolio costs $108 plus $37.50, or $145.50.\n\nM1 falls short of Stash on perks. The Stash Plan includes a 3% retirement match, a $10,000 life insurance policy and Stock-Back rewards. M1's pricing lists none of those three."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Fidelity Go Fee Threshold at $25,000",
      "body": "Fidelity Go charges no advisory fee on balances under $25,000 and 0.35% a year at $25,000 and above. Fidelity Go has no minimum to open, and investing starts at $10, since the balance must be at least $10 to invest. Accounts can be an IRA, an HSA or a taxable account, invested in a mix of mutual funds.\n\nFrom $25,000, Fidelity Go adds unlimited 1-on-1 financial coaching calls. Tax-loss harvesting also starts at $25,000, on taxable accounts in Flex Funds holding stocks.\n\nFidelity Go fits people with balances under $25,000 who want a managed account with no advisory fee. As an illustration, a $20,000 Fidelity Go account pays $0 in advisory fees, while a $20,000 Stash Smart Portfolio costs $108 plus $50, or $158, on annual billing. At $30,000, Fidelity Go costs 0.35% of $30,000, or $105, against $108 plus $75, or $183, at Stash.\n\nFidelity Go falls short of Stash at large balances and on perks. Above $108,000, the 0.35% rate costs more than a Stash Smart Portfolio's $108 plus 0.25% on annual billing. At $120,000, for example, Fidelity Go costs $420 a year and the Stash Smart Portfolio costs $108 plus $300, or $408.\n\nFidelity Go lists no retirement match, life insurance policy or stock rewards. The Stash Plan includes all three for $108 a year on annual billing."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Who This Is Not For",
      "body": "Each option on this list leaves some buyers out, and each has a listed alternative.\n\n- Stash fits poorly for day traders, because Stash says it is built for long-term investing. Robinhood Gold lists futures and index options pricing instead.\n- Stash fits poorly for large Smart Portfolio balances when the perks go unused. At $50,000, Stash costs $233 a year on annual billing against $125 at Wealthfront's 0.25% fee.\n- Acorns Bronze fits poorly for people who want an IRA match, since the match starts at Silver. Acorns Silver lists a 1% first-year match and Gold lists 3%.\n- Betterment Premium fits poorly for accounts under $100,000, the listed minimum. Betterment's standard investing accounts carry no minimum balance.\n- Robinhood Gold fits poorly for people who may cancel Gold within a year or move IRA assets within five years, because the full match requires both. Stash describes its 3% match as worth up to $225 per year with no listed holding period.\n- Fidelity Go fits poorly for people who want coaching calls on balances under $25,000, because coaching starts at $25,000. Stash includes money coaching from a registered investment advisor at any balance."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Would Change Our Answer on Stash Alternatives",
      "body": "These published price or feature moves would change the comparisons above:\n\n- Stash changing The Stash Plan from $12 per month or $108 per year.\n- Stash removing or changing the 0.25% advisory fee on Smart Portfolios of $1,000 or more, which would close the $108 gap with Wealthfront.\n- Acorns extending its Gold 3% IRA match beyond new contributions in the first year.\n- Betterment changing the $200 monthly deposit or $24,000 balance thresholds that switch its $5 fee to 0.25%.\n- Robinhood changing the $5 Gold price or the one-year and five-year conditions on its 3% IRA match.\n- M1 moving its platform fee waiver from more than $10,000.\n- Fidelity Go moving its $25,000 threshold or its 0.35% rate, which would move the $108,000 break-even against Stash.\n- Any change to the variable cash rates at Acorns, Betterment, Robinhood or M1."
    }
