  "stillBetterFit": {
    "heading": "When Stash Is Still the Better Fit",
    "body": "Stash fits people who want guidance, a retirement match, life insurance and stock rewards under one flat subscription. The Stash Plan costs $108 billed annually and includes a 3% retirement match worth up to $225 per year and Stock-Back rewards worth up to $120 per year. Those two listed maximums add up to $345, against the $108 annual price.\n\nThe plan also includes 24/7 financial guidance, a personalized investment plan, a $10,000 life insurance policy, budgeting tools and direct deposit up to 2 days early. Expert money coaching comes from a registered investment advisor, and Stash is registered with the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) as an investment advisor. Banking runs through Stride Bank N.A., a member of the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC).\n\nStash lists fractional shares from $5 or less across thousands of stocks and ETFs, plus custodial accounts for kids. Stash says it has over 1 million investors, offers a 7-day trial and allows cancellation any time. Stash also lists no banking overdraft charges and no add-on transaction fees."
  },
  "verdict": "Stash costs $108 a year on annual billing plus 0.25% on Smart Portfolios of $1,000 or more, and its perks list up to $345 a year in combined match and Stock-Back value. Wealthfront and Fidelity Go cost less on fees alone at most balances, Robinhood Gold offers the same size of IRA match for $60 a year, Acorns Gold matches the Stash monthly price with checking and a Will, and M1 covers the widest range of account types.\n\nFor Stash alternatives, the concrete next step is to write down the balance you plan to invest and your monthly deposit, then read the matching row in the table above.",
